  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nA much cooler day, in comparison to the past few oppressive heat\ndays, is on display across the Mid-South with current\ntemperatures in the 80s to lower 90s thanks to yesterday's cold\nfront. This front has stalled just south of the Tennessee and\nMississippi state line warranting a Heat Advisory remains in\neffect for the Mississippi Delta Region and has been extended\nacross portions of north Mississippi through 8PM. A warm and\nhumid airmass remains in place across the Mid-South leading to\nmuggy conditions as dewpoints remain in the mid to upper 70s\nnorth of this front and upper 70s to lower 80s south. The latest\nKNQA radar sweep indicates very light radar returns north of this\nfront with enough instability (~2500 J/kg of SBCAPE, 30 kts of\neffective shear, and PWATs around 2.1\") to support a few weak\nthunderstorms over north Mississippi. As we move into the\nafternoon hours, the parameter spacing will remain about the same\nsouth of the Tennessee and Mississippi state line. Severe weather\nis not expected today, though, the parameter space may support a\nfew stronger diurnally driven thunderstorms with heavy rainfall\nrates and gusty winds.\n\nBy tomorrow, dewpoints will continue to decrease as PWATs also\nfall below 2.0\" for much of our area. Though the Mississippi\nDelta region may still retain dewpoints in the upper 70s, being\nour hot spot again tomorrow, a Heat Advisory is not anticipated.\nDecreased PWATs and a less humid airmass will also support an\noverall dry day with mostly clear skies and highs in the upper\n80s to lower 90s tomorrow. Dry conditions will not linger very\nlong as by Friday, a weak upper-level trough and a surface low\nwill begin moving into the Upper Mississippi Valley. An attendant\ncold front is also expected to begin edging closer to our area\nFriday morning with isolated to scattered shower and thunderstorm\nchances out ahead of it. By the evening hours Friday, this\npositively tilted will continue to push east into the Ohio River\nValley with the attendant cold front beginning to push across the\nMid-South. Shower and thunderstorm chances will become more\nwidespread in nature and linger into the early evening hours\nSaturday. Digging into the parameter spacing Friday into\nSaturday, this system is shaping up to be more of a potential\nheavy rainfall than severe event, as forecast PWATs surge to\n2.2\"+ and the latest LREF has <10% of >500 J/kg of SBCAPE and\n25 kts of 0-6km bulk wind shear. We will continue to monitor the\noverall trend of this system as details become more clear.\n\nThe aforementioned trough will continue migrating east through\nthe weekend with a few pop-up showers along the Tennessee River\nand pleasant temperatures in the 80s. Moving into next workweek,\ntemperatures will trend near normal with highs in the upper 80s\nto lower 90s and lowered dewpoints in the upper 60s to lower 70s\nas ensembles are hinting at near north upper-level flow and an\noverall dry airmass following behind the aforementioned trough\nand front.\n\nAEH",
